[
    {
        "content": "<p>For a submodule of a function type, when should we add <code>CoeFun</code> and when should we just use <code>.val</code>? This question grows out from the review of <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43948\">#43948</a>.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi : G \\to H</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ϕ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span></span></span></span> be a group morphism and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\rho, V)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ρ</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> be a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span>-representation of G. The (underlying <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span>-module of the) coinduced representation is defined to be </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">coindV</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">)}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Mathlib's <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RepresentationTheory/Coinduced.html#Representation.coindV\">Representation.coindV</a> has type <code>Submodule k H \\to V</code>. I pretty much regard terms of <code>coindV</code> literally as functions in mind, just as how we talk about this definition and do calculations in paper. Without the <code>CoeFun</code> instance, writing <code>f.val x</code> all the time makes me uncomfortable and feel <code>coindV</code> being a subtype, but in my mind <code>coindV</code> should be a very primitive concept for <code>RepresentationTheory</code>. In <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43948\">#43948</a>, I proposed to add</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CoeFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">coindV</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">Subtype</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"699016\">@Edison Xie</span> pointed out that \"there is no general coe instance for elements in submodules to the ambient module\" and suggested to open this Zulip discussion.</p>\n<p>Another way to do this to add an <code>FunLike</code> instance, which was used in <a href=\"https://github.com/kbuzzard/ClassFieldTheory/blob/main/ClassFieldTheory/Cohomology/IndCoind/Finite.lean\">class field theory</a> <code>IndCoind/Finite.lean</code> line 79-83</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">coind₁V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">coindV</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Subgroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">subtype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FunLike</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">coind₁V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe_injective</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Subtype</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val_injective</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I want to remark that using <code>FunLike</code> for <code>coindV</code> breaks some proofs because various definitions internally use <code>.val</code> so that we have to manage <code>f x</code> v.s. <code>f.val x</code>, e.g. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Module/Submodule/LinearMap.html#LinearMap.coe_restrict_apply\">LinearMap.coe_restrict_apply</a> needs <code>(foo.restrict _ _).val</code>.</p>",
        "id": 629863792,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1791382366
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can you please link this discussion thread to the PR description as well? Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 630023891,
        "sender_full_name": "Edison Xie",
        "timestamp": 1791431608
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't think <code>CoeFun</code> is the right comparison; it is inlined, so using CoeFun <em>is</em> the same as <code>.val</code></p>",
        "id": 630025262,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1791432322
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If the question is <code>DFunLike.coe</code> vs <code>Subtype.val</code>; this is indeed an annoyance, as you end up having to repeat lemmas like <code>coe (f + g) = coe f + coe g</code> for both coercions</p>",
        "id": 630025445,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1791432457
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"310045\">Eric Wieser</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/.60CoeFun.60.20or.20.60.2Eval.60/near/630025262\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I don't think <code>CoeFun</code> is the right comparison; it is inlined, so using CoeFun <em>is</em> the same as <code>.val</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Sorry I don't get it. My point is that <code>CoeFun</code> enables the notation <code>f x</code>, which is surely the same as <code>f.val x</code>. But without <code>CoeFun</code>, you can't write <code>f x</code>.</p>",
        "id": 630026437,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1791433051
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"310045\">Eric Wieser</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/.60CoeFun.60.20or.20.60.2Eval.60/near/630025445\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If the question is <code>DFunLike.coe</code> vs <code>Subtype.val</code>; this is indeed an annoyance, as you end up having to repeat lemmas like <code>coe (f + g) = coe f + coe g</code> for both coercions</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It's not likely that we are going to switch to <code>FunLike</code> in this PR exactly because of this. My point is that <code>FunLike</code> also enables the notation <code>f x</code> in class field theory repo, so why not enable this notation for <code>coindV</code>?</p>",
        "id": 630027128,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1791433462
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't object this change per se, but why is <code>f x</code> necessarily better than <code>f.val x</code>? To me it seems you lose the information that <code>f</code> come from a submodule</p>",
        "id": 630027219,
        "sender_full_name": "Edison Xie",
        "timestamp": 1791433530
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here's what I meant:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyF</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">//</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyF</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">section</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">local</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CoeFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyF</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Subtype</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- these two produce identical terms</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_expr</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=ₛ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">section</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">local</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FunLike</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyF</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Subtype</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe_injective</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Subtype</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val_injective</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- but now we're in trouble</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_expr</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=ₛ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">val</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 630027262,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1791433566
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"448405\">@Alex Meiburg</span>, perhaps worth noting that Physlib's <code>HermitianMat</code> runs into this issue.</p>",
        "id": 630027409,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1791433685
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1097608\">Jiaxi Mo</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/.60CoeFun.60.20or.20.60.2Eval.60/near/629863792\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I want to remark that using <code>FunLike</code> for <code>coindV</code> breaks some proofs because various definitions internally use <code>.val</code> so that we have to manage <code>f x</code> v.s. <code>f.val x</code>, e.g. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Module/Submodule/LinearMap.html#LinearMap.coe_restrict_apply\">LinearMap.coe_restrict_apply</a> needs <code>(foo.restrict _ _).val</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"310045\">@Eric Wieser</span>  I suppose we are talking about the same thing</p>",
        "id": 630030288,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1791434939
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"699016\">Edison Xie</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/.60CoeFun.60.20or.20.60.2Eval.60/near/630027219\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I don't object this change per se, but why is <code>f x</code> necessarily better than <code>f.val x</code>? To me it seems you lose the information that <code>f</code> come from a submodule</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can still and <strong><em>have to</em></strong> use <code>f.val</code> <em>alone</em> to record it coming from a submodule. But I also regard <code>f</code> as a function if it is followed by a variable , namely <code>f x</code>.</p>",
        "id": 630030657,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1791435202
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Moreover, why do you think it should come from a submodule? I don't see a representation theoretic reason. Being a submodule is mainly an implementation result, not intrinsic for coinduced representations. I mean another implementation <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RepresentationTheory/Coinduced.html#Representation.coind'\">coind'</a> in mathlib uses <code>Hom_{k[G]}(k[H], V)</code> as the underlying module. It won't let you feel it is a submodule of <code>Hom_k(k[H], V)</code> at all. </p>\n<p>I disagree the philosophy that you should let the user feel the implementation details all the time for such fundamental objects which can be mathematically characterised by universal property through adjunction. That's also the reason that I am strongly against the current design of <code>IndV</code> and <code>ind</code>: it leaks the implementation everywhere.</p>",
        "id": 630121828,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1791460831
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If that's the case, are you saying we should all switch to <code>coind'</code> and get rid of the current <code>IndV</code> and <code>ind</code>?</p>",
        "id": 630139925,
        "sender_full_name": "Edison Xie",
        "timestamp": 1791464831
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>coind'</code>: I am not saying we should, since <code>CoeFun</code> is satisfactory to me, but I am not object to do an experiment which requires some effort, e.g. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RepresentationTheory/Coinduced.html#Representation.coind'\">Representation.coind'</a> using <code>Rep.res</code> seems to be wrong to me.</p>\n<p><code>IndV</code> and <code>ind</code>: definitely I want to get rid of the current <code>ind</code> API <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43949\">#43949</a>. I can't believe the <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RepresentationTheory/Induced.html#Representation.ind_apply\">following lemma</a> is the desired user interface.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Representation</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ind_apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_1</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_3</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_4</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Representation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"